Template: homelab-postfix/hostname
Type: string
Default: mail.test.mailmasker.org
Description: SMTP hostname (myhostname):
 Appears in the HELO/EHLO banner and outbound Received headers.

Template: homelab-postfix/mail_domain
Type: string
Default: test.mailmasker.org
Description: Mail domain this server is authoritative for (virtual_mailbox_domains):
 Must match the domain part of the email addresses homelab-api issues,
 or valid accounts will be rejected as unknown recipients.

Template: homelab-postfix/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host to bootstrap the runtime role on:
 Used once, at install time, to create the narrow homelab_postfix_runtime
 role and grant it column-scoped SELECT on api.users (see
 script/homelab-postfix-bootstrap-role). Needs direct Postgres access
 (peer auth as the postgres OS user, locally or over SSH) — this is NOT
 the host Postfix connects to at runtime, see pgbouncer_host below.

Template: homelab-postfix/pgbouncer_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PgBouncer host for the runtime recipient-validation query:
 Postfix's virtual_mailbox_maps pgsql lookup connects here at runtime
 (port 6432), never straight to Postgres — see CLAUDE.md's split
 runtime/migrate role design. The bootstrapped role is also registered
 into this host's pgbouncer userlist.txt.

Template: homelab-postfix/dovecot_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: Dovecot host for LMTP delivery and SASL auth:
 Both virtual_transport (LMTP, port 24) and smtpd_sasl_path (port
 12345) point here over plain TCP — this package no longer assumes
 Dovecot is on the same host. Matches this project's own production
 mail stack, where Postfix and Dovecot run on separate hosts and talk
 over exactly these two ports (confirmed against the real production
 config, not assumed). The default of localhost preserves today's
 co-located behavior for anyone who doesn't touch this.

Template: homelab-postfix/default_process_limit
Type: string
Default: 600
Description: Postfix default_process_limit (total concurrent processes per service, e.g. smtpd):
 Postfix's own stock default is 100 -- never explicitly set by this
 package before, found live to be comfortably below what these
 containers' CPU/RAM can actually sustain. Sized to stay above
 homelab-haproxy's smtp_in/submission_in frontend maxconn (default 500
 in that package) so Postfix's own total capacity is never the first
 thing to bind for traffic HAProxy already decided to accept.

Template: homelab-postfix/client_connection_limit
Type: string
Default: 200
Description: Postfix smtpd_client_connection_count_limit (max concurrent connections from one client IP):
 Postfix's own stock default is 50. Confirmed live as the real cause of
 a stress-test mail-send collapse: HAProxy's send-proxy preserves the
 real client IP, so a burst of concurrent connections from one real
 sender hits this per-client cap long before the server's total
 capacity does. Kept as a bounded fraction of default_process_limit
 (above) rather than raised to match it 1:1 or disabled -- a single
 client still shouldn't be able to monopolize the whole process pool.

Template: homelab-postfix/behind_haproxy
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Put this Postfix's SMTP/submission traffic behind HAProxy (homelab-haproxy)?
 Recommended, and the default: matches this project's own production
 mail stack (Postfix and Dovecot each sit behind a real HAProxy,
 confirmed live), and it's the setup that stays correct no matter where
 this host ends up -- one box today, split across two tomorrow, with
 nothing to reconfigure at the mail-transport layer either way. Set up
 homelab-haproxy in front of this host (locally, proxying to
 127.0.0.1, if this is a single-box install; on whichever host holds
 the public IP, if split) to actually receive connections once this is
 answered "true".
 .
 Answering "true" enables smtpd_upstream_proxy_protocol = haproxy,
 which then REQUIRES a PROXY-protocol preamble on every connection --
 confirmed live: until HAProxy is actually in front of this host and
 sending that preamble (send-proxy on its backend line), every direct
 SMTP connection is rejected outright with "421 Server local error"
 (the peer times out waiting for a proxy header that never arrives).
 Answer "false" only for a deliberately direct, unproxied setup (e.g.
 while still setting up homelab-haproxy) -- dpkg-reconfigure
 homelab-postfix to change this later either way, without a full
 reinstall.

Template: homelab-postfix/enable_dkim
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Wire DKIM signing into Postfix (OpenDKIM milter)?
 Adds smtpd_milters/non_smtpd_milters pointing at OpenDKIM (see
 dkim_milter_host below -- OpenDMARC is not wired here, out of scope
 for this package). An already-configured host answering "false" here
 (the default) gets zero milter-config change on a routine upgrade --
 turn this on explicitly via `dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix` when
 ready. Key generation/rotation itself is done separately via
 `homelab-cli dns dkim rotate/activate/retire`, not by this postinst.

Template: homelab-postfix/run_local_opendkim
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Run OpenDKIM locally on THIS host (only asked when enable_dkim is true)?
 True (the default) is the normal, single-instance setup: this host
 runs its own OpenDKIM, homelab-domain-admin must also be installed
 here (DKIM private key material is read from local disk only and must
 never cross a network -- see domain-admin/README.md), and
 /etc/opendkim/keys ownership is set up so domain-admin's unprivileged
 `homelab` user can generate/rotate keys there.
 .
 Answer "false" to run multiple load-balanced Postfix instances signing
 with the SAME DKIM key/selector, instead of each instance rotating its
 own (which would mean several different selectors published in DNS at
 once for no real benefit): exactly one instance in the group answers
 "true" here (the "primary", co-located with homelab-domain-admin as
 usual) and every other instance ("secondary") answers "false" and
 instead points dkim_milter_host below at the primary's own address --
 nothing DKIM-related is installed, started, or given key-directory
 access on a "false" host, it just relays signing requests to the
 primary's milter over the network.

Template: homelab-postfix/dkim_milter_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: Host Postfix connects to for DKIM signing (OpenDKIM's milter, port 8891):
 Almost always leave this at "localhost" -- the normal setup is
 Postfix and OpenDKIM co-located, talking over loopback, which is also
 what run_local_opendkim=true expects. Only change this on a
 "secondary" instance in a load-balanced group (run_local_opendkim=
 false): point it at the "primary" instance's own data-plane address
 (e.g. 10.50.2.46) so this host's outbound mail still gets signed, by
 the one shared OpenDKIM, over the network instead of locally. The
 primary's own firewall needs an inbound allowance for port 8891 from
 wherever its secondaries live before this will actually work.

Template: homelab-postfix/dkim_milter_bind_address
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: Address THIS host's own OpenDKIM binds its milter socket to (only read when run_local_opendkim is true):
 Leave at "localhost" for a normal single-instance setup (nothing
 outside this host ever needs to reach its milter). Set this to
 "0.0.0.0" (this project's usual "bind broadly, the firewall is the
 real boundary" convention) ONLY on a "primary" host in a load-balanced
 group, i.e. one that other Postfix instances' dkim_milter_host above
 will be pointing at -- otherwise OpenDKIM only accepts connections
 from 127.0.0.1 and every remote secondary's mail silently stops being
 signed (milter_default_action=accept means it still gets DELIVERED,
 just unsigned, which is easy to miss without checking).

Template: homelab-postfix/instance_id
Type: string
Default:
Description: Unique identifier for this HA instance's own Postgres role:
 Only needed when running more than one homelab-postfix instance behind
 the same HAProxy pool (see homelab-haproxy's own backends.yml) -- each
 instance needs its OWN Postgres role, or every new instance's install
 silently rotates the shared role's password out from under every other
 already-running instance (confirmed live: this broke SMTP submission
 and RCPT TO validation fleet-wide during a real 3-instance rebuild).
 Leave blank for a single, standalone instance (keeps the plain
 homelab_postfix_runtime role name, matching every install before this
 template existed). For an HA pool, give each instance its own short,
 stable value (e.g. its own hostname: "ct02", "ct13", "ct14") -- the
 role becomes homelab_postfix_runtime_<value>. Changing this on an
 already-bootstrapped instance does NOT migrate the old role; run
 'dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix' afterward to bootstrap the new one.
